      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14162111/assets/a3e52270e83d29fe4d53f1429a6648c6ef2451ae5ad5b3d48dd0d1a0f3450df4.png)\n\nWhat are the $xy$-coordinates of the point shown in the axes above?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point is $3$ units to the right of the origin, in the positive direction of the $x$-axis. So, the point has an $x$-coordinate of $3.$\n\nAdditionally, the point is $6$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, the point has a $y$-coordinate of $-6.$\n\nThe point has an $x$-coordinate of $3$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-6,$ so its coordinates are $(3,-6).$",
